1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,550
In 1974, America tried to lock the clock. Permanently.

2
00:00:04,830 --> 00:00:13,190
Gas lines ran for miles. So Congress made daylight saving time year-round — to save 150,000 barrels of oil a day.

3
00:00:13,430 --> 00:00:17,600
On January sixth, the clocks jumped ahead. But the sun didn't.

4
00:00:17,880 --> 00:00:24,100
Sunrise slid past eight in the morning. In Detroit, Minneapolis, and Seattle — nine o'clock, or later.

5
00:00:24,340 --> 00:00:32,300
Children walked to school in the dark. In Florida, eight schoolchildren died that January. The year before: two.

6
00:00:32,580 --> 00:00:38,750
Parents called it Daylight Disaster Time. Approval collapsed from 79 percent to 42 — in eight weeks.

7
00:00:38,990 --> 00:00:44,450
The promised oil savings? Around one percent. And even that number was disputed.

8
00:00:44,690 --> 00:00:51,920
Ten months later, Congress reversed course. October 27th, 1974 — the clocks rolled back.

9
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:55,550
Fifty-two years later, the House has voted to lock the clock again.

10
00:00:55,790 --> 00:00:58,500
The sun hasn't changed. Only the argument has.
